  "client_view_text": " Here's a text file with a few lines of data:\n```\n1\tMike\n2\tJane\n3\tBob\n```\nSuppose we are writing to this file and part of the way through, the system crashes. When the system comes back up, the file might look like this:\n```\n1\tMike\n2\tJane\n3\tXXX\n```\nWhere the `XXX` is some garbage data.\n\nTo recover from this, a write-ahead log is used. The log contains a record of all the changes that we've made, in the order that they were made. So, if we've written to Write a log record in the format required to write it to the log file. \n\n## Step 1: Understanding the Role of a Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log is a critical component of a database system that ensures data integrity and facilitates recovery in the event of a crash or failure. Its primary function is to record all changes made to the database before applying them to the actual database files.\n\n## Step 2: How Write-Ahead Log Facilitates Recovery\nWhen a database transaction is committed, the changes are first written to the log. Then, the changes are applied to the main database files. This ensures that, even if the database crashes before the changes can be fully applied, the log contains a record of all changes that need to be reapplied to the database to bring it to a consistent state. By replaying the log after recovery from a crash, the database can restore its original state.\n\n## Step 3: Writing a Log Record\nA log record typically includes a timestamp, a transaction ID, and the type of operation (insert, update, delete), along with any necessary data. For example, if we're inserting a record with ID 1, name \"John Doe\", and email \"john@example.com\", a log record might look"
